{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And they offered before HaShem foreign fire which He had not commanded them.</b> As for the nature of this sin, the opinions are many. In the Yalkut (Shemini, sec. 524) it concludes in the name of Rabbi Mani that it was because they entered intoxicated with wine, and because they entered without washing hands and feet, and some say they lacked garments, namely the tunic, and some say because they had no children, and some say because they had not taken wives, and some say because they ruled a halakhah in the presence of Moses their teacher, and some say because they used to walk about saying: When will these two old men die, and I and you will rule the community? And some say that the sin of the calf that Aaron made caused it for them, and they expounded on this the verse ״יְשַׁלֵּם שְׁנַיִם לְרֵעֵהוּ״ (Exodus 22:8). And all these opinions, even though they have some support from other verses — nevertheless this verse refutes all those opinions, for it says: ״וַיַּקְרִיבוּ לִפְנֵי ה׳ אֵשׁ זָרָה אֲשֶׁר לֹא צִוָּה אוֹתָם״ — hear from it that there was no other sin in them except this one." "Therefore the obligation of explanation falls upon me to plead on behalf of all these opinions, and to say that all of them found for themselves support from this very verse — because it was difficult for them: is it not already stated (Leviticus 1:7), ״וְנָתְנוּ בְּנֵי אַהֲרֹן הַכֹּהֲנִים אֵשׁ״ etc., that it is a mitzvah to bring fire from the ordinary person, so how can it say ״אֲשֶׁר לֹא צִוָּה אוֹתָם״? And one cannot say according to the words of Mahar[i'a] that they should have taken the fire of the incense from the altar of burnt offering, for indeed that command was after the death of Aaron's sons, and there it says (Leviticus 16:12), ״וְלָקַח מְלֹא הַמַּחְתָּה גַּחֲלֵי אֵשׁ מֵעַל הַמִּזְבֵּחַ מִלִּפְנֵי ה׳״ etc. Therefore it is fitting to explain this foreign fire by way of foreignness and allusion to other things that were alluded to in the foreign fire." "For the opinion of the one who says they entered intoxicated with wine — he holds that this foreign fire speaks of the wine that burns in a man like fire, as it is written (Isaiah 5:11), ״מְאַחֲרֵי בַנֶּשֶׁף יַיִן יַדְלִיקֵם״, and it is literally a flame from the burning that the wine kindled within them. And even though the passage ״יַיִן וְשֵׁכָר אַל תֵּשְׁתְּ״ had not yet been stated to them, nevertheless by the principle of ״הַקְרִיבֵהוּ נָא לְפֶחָתֶךָ״ they should have known by reasoning that one does not enter the gate of the King while drunk. And a great proof for this opinion is from what is written (Exodus 24:11), ״וְאֶל אֲצִילֵי בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל לֹא שָׁלַח יָדוֹ״ — the inference being that they were worthy of having a hand sent against them, for having eaten and drunk and let their eyes stray from the Shekhinah through the coarse heart of eating and drinking. And our Sages of blessed memory said (Leviticus Rabbah 20:10) that the Holy One, blessed be He, did not wish to mix in with the rejoicing of the Torah and waited for them until this day. And the question is: what distinguishes this day from others? And moreover, surely this day too is called ״שִׂמְחַת לִבּוֹ״, for thus our Sages of blessed memory expounded (Ta'anit 26b): ״בְּיוֹם חֲתֻנָּתוֹ״ — this is the giving of the Torah; ״וּבְיוֹם שִׂמְחַת לִבּוֹ״ — this is the dedication of the Temple, so He should have waited still longer. But rather, because on this day they reverted and repeated their folly and offered before HaShem foreign fire, which burned within them from the side of wine, and out of the abundance of drinking they came before HaShem in an unlawful manner — therefore HaShem did not wish to wait for them any longer, so that the matter not seem in their eyes as permitted, for one who transgresses a transgression and repeats it, it becomes permitted to him (Yoma 86b). And therefore it was said to Aaron after the death of his two sons the command ״בְּזֹאת יָבֹא אַהֲרֹן אֶל הַקֹּדֶשׁ״ etc. (Leviticus 16:3) on the day of Yom Kippur, a day of afflicting one's soul, for then he comes with humility before his Creator, not out of a coarse heart — for wine is a faithless messenger, a haughty man who does not rest (Habakkuk 2:5)." "And according to the one who says they had not taken wives — the fire of desire was burning within them, for every man who is unmarried is full of thoughts of transgression from foreign fire, and they sinned with boiling [passions], and with boiling [passions] they were judged (Sanhedrin 108a). And so we find in the Gemara: a fire in the house of Amram etc. (Kiddushin 81a) — regarding the craving for women that burns within a man like fire in flax." "And according to the one who says they were lacking the tunic — it is known that the tunic atones for evil tongue (Arakhin 16a), and these spread evil tongue about Moses and Aaron, saying: When will these two old men die etc. — by this they meant that they are not fit to be leaders except by virtue of their old age, not by virtue of their merit. And every speaker of evil tongue offers foreign fire — coals burn from him — as it is written (Proverbs 26:18), ״כְּמִתְלַהְלֵהַּ הַיּוֹרֶה זִקִּים חִצִּים וָמָוֶת״, and that is זִיקֵי דְּנוּר — sparks of fire — for his arrows go forth like lightning, and therefore his punishment is by fire, as it is said (Psalms 120:3–4), ״מַה יִּתֵּן לְךָ וּמַה יֹּסִיף לָךְ לָשׁוֹן רְמִיָּה, חִצֵּי גִבּוֹר שְׁנוּנִים עִם גַּחֲלֵי רְתָמִים״. And therefore they too were judged by fire. But had they been wearing the tunic, it would have atoned for them regarding the evil tongue. And the analogy of fire encompasses also the trait of arrogance, for every arrogant person wishes to rise upward like the nature of fire which rises upward, and therefore his punishment is by fire, as explained above in parashat Tzav on the statement of our Sages of blessed memory: ״הִוא הָעֹלָה עַל מוֹקְדָה״ — whoever is arrogant is judged by fire (Leviticus Rabbah 7:6). And Aaron's sons made their hearts coarse through this offering, and that is the foreign fire. And this matter encompasses many opinions: whether the opinion of the one who says they were intoxicated with wine, which is through a coarse heart; whether the opinion of the one who says they did not take wives, because of the abundance of their arrogance in thinking that they had no equal upon the dust; whether the opinion of the one who says they did not take counsel one from the other, because of the abundance of their arrogance; whether the opinion of the one who says they ruled a halakhah in the presence of Moses their teacher, for out of much arrogance they did not take permission from Moses; whether the opinion of the one who says they said וַאֲנִי וְאַתָּה נַנְהִיג שְׂרָרָה etc." "And the one who says that they had no children — it appears that he too holds with the opinion of the midrash that says that the sin of the calf was called to account upon them. For in idolatry the measure is thus: the Holy One, blessed be He, calls to account the sin of fathers upon children and upon children's children (Exodus 34:7), and for Aaron's honor the Holy One, blessed be He, would lengthen His anger and lift it from his grandchildren; but since they had no children, the sin was called to account upon them.\nAnd perhaps at the time of setting the strange fire they were reflecting on the fire of the calf, for Aaron had thrown the gold there and this calf emerged, and they thought that through that strange fire that miracle was performed — thus they reflected in setting this fire, that a miracle would be performed for them through it. And HaShem knew the thoughts of their heart, for strange fire — that is what they were offering before Him; therefore He judged them with fire, in the manner stated concerning the calf: ״וַיִּשְׂרֹף אֹתוֹ בָאֵשׁ״ (Exodus 32:20)." "And the opinion of the one who says that they had not washed their hands and feet holds that that is the strange fire — for because they had not sanctified their hands and feet from the basin, they were profane in the place of holiness. And therefore the fire was called 'strange fire,' for strangeness (זָרוּת) is the opposite of holiness (קְדֻשָּׁה), as it is written (Leviticus 22:10): ״וְכָל זָר לֹא יֹאכַל קֹדֶשׁ.״ Therefore it says ״אִישׁ מַחְתָּתוֹ״ — as though the pan were his own, in which there was no aspect of holiness, and all this because they had not sanctified their hands and feet. And because they sinned through water, therefore they were judged by fire, which prevails in a place where no water is available to extinguish it."], :book :leviticus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :notes {0 "The Yalkut Shim'oni section number תקנד in the Hebrew renders as 524 in standard numerals (ת+ק+נ+ד = 400+100+50+4).", 2 "Mahar[i'a] left unresolved: the abbreviation מהרי״א could refer to several commentators; left as transliteration without expanding the name."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:36:07.062289691Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}